{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>It is clean, and the priest shall pronounce it clean.</b> In Torat Kohanim they said: ״Clean״ — perhaps he may declare it clean and go on his way? Scripture says: ״and the priest shall pronounce it clean.״ Perhaps if a priest said about an unclean one ״clean,״ it should be clean? Scripture says: ״clean, and the priest shall pronounce it clean.״ And on this matter Hillel, peace be upon him, went up from Babylonia (Jerusalem Talmud, Pesachim 6:1). This implies that he could not compel them from the verse until he went up to the Land of Israel and received that such is indeed the halakhah, as he expounded. And one needs to know on what basis those who disagreed with him interpreted the verses." "<b>And it shall appear</b> that ״clean״ and ״shall pronounce it clean״ are needed to establish that he may not declare it clean and go on his way, but rather until the priest says to him: ״You are clean״ — for without the word ״clean,״ I would have said that since the person himself does not know, a priest should come and inform him, for the priest is the expert in these halakhot and ״they shall seek Torah from his mouth,״ and in any case if the person himself knows that it is a bohaq he may declare it clean and go; therefore Scripture says: ״it is clean, and the priest shall pronounce it clean.״ And there is no need for the exposition of Hillel — that he had to go up to the Land of Israel and know that such is the halakhah. From here on, let us return to establish the halakhah from the verse in this manner: ״it is clean״ — specifically if it is in truth clean; in that case the priest's words are effective; but if he declared an unclean one clean, it does not help. And do not find it difficult — perhaps the verse intended, in saying ״clean,״ that even if he himself knows it is clean, [still the priest must declare it]; but that is only a rejection, and it would be valid before clarification of the truth, since such is the halakhah that came down. However, once the law that we establish through inference is the halakhah, it is preferable to say that the verse intended this rather than to push away rejections. And this is a correct general principle in the interpretation of the Torah." "And I have seen fit to explain the statements of our Sages of blessed memory, who said (Leviticus Rabbah 22:1) that there was nothing that was not transmitted to Moses at Sinai, even what a veteran disciple is destined to innovate in the future. And they said elsewhere (Numbers Rabbah 19:6) that Rabbi Akiva would expound what Moses did not know, as it says: ״the things I have done״ — ״I shall do״ was not said, etc.; one should examine their words there. And likewise several statements similar to this." "<b>And it shall appear</b> that the resolution of these statements is as follows: it is true that every matter of Torah was said to Moses, and no sage can know more than what Moses knew — and even if you combine all the generations of Israel from the day the Torah was given until the earth is filled with knowledge, there is no innovation that Moses did not know. But the distinction is that HaShem gave Moses the Written Torah and the Oral Torah, and behold the Master, blessed be He, in His wisdom, blessed be He, inscribed in the Written Torah all of the Oral Torah that He told Moses — but He did not inform Moses of all that He gave him orally, where it is alluded to in the Written Torah. And this is the labor of the children of Israel, toilers in Torah: to clothe the halakhot that were stated to Moses at Sinai, and the secrets and the derashot — all of them — to give them a place in the Written Torah. And therefore you find that the Tannaim came and composed Torat Kohanim and Sifrei, etc., and all their derashot in the verses are nothing but based on the halakhot, and they clothed them in the perfect Torah of HaShem that is in writing. And after them, to this very day, this is the sacred labor of the children of Torah: to examine the verses precisely and to align them with the statements that are the Oral Torah. And this is the labor of Torah called the Land of the Living. And this matter was not transmitted to Moses in its entirety — to know where all of the Oral Torah is alluded to in the Written Torah. And therefore they said, of blessed memory, that Rabbi Akiva expounded derashot that Moses did not know: the intention is not that Moses did not know the essence of the matters — for everything is from him, even what a veteran disciple is destined to innovate — but rather that he did not know their support and precise derivation, where they are alluded to in the Torah. And here is your sign from what stands before us: Hillel expounded from the verse the halakhah that was stated to Moses orally, and HaShem did not reveal to Moses its basis in the Written Torah, and Hillel came and expounded it. And these matters are correct."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The Jerusalem Talmud passage cited describes Hillel ascending from Babylonia with a tradition that the disagreement could not be resolved from Scripture alone."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:18.716962831Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}